<td id="S4.T2.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Thermal Violations</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="204\pm 201.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">204</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">±</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">201.72</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">204\pm 201.72</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{184.33\pm 69.47}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold">184.33</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">±</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold">69.47</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{184.33\pm 69.47}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Avg. CPU Usage (%)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.84\pm 13.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">18.84</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">±</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">13.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.84\pm 13.01</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{19.72\pm 17.43}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold">19.72</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">±</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold">17.43</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{19.72\pm 17.43}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The mixed performance profile can be partially explained by a temporal mismatch between the LLM decision cycle and the orbital thermal dynamics, combined with the stabilizing effect of the agentic policy on thermal control. Unlike terrestrial experiments, where ambient temperature evolves gradually and predictably, the ISS undergoes rapid and extreme thermal transitions due to its 90-minute orbital period. As the station circles the Earth, it experiences approximately 45 minutes of direct sunlight followed by 45 minutes of eclipse, resulting in sharp temperature swings across its external surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, the analysis window provided to the LLM was shortened to 15 minutes to approximate this orbital cadence. However, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.1 Ground Experiments ‣ 4 Results &amp; Analysis ‣ ASTREA: Introducing Agentic Intelligence for Orbital Thermal Autonomy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the LLM required up to 8 minutes to generate a recommendation in the current configuration. During such an interval, the ISS may already undergo a complete sun/eclipse transition, rendering the suggested <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> value outdated at the moment of application. Consequently, some updates introduced exploratory behavior precisely during phases that demanded conservative exploitation, while in other cases the agentic adjustments reduced the magnitude and frequency of thermal violations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the mixed outcomes observed in orbit, the successful deployment and execution of the agentic system aboard the ISS validates the feasibility of the onboard architecture under real flight conditions. This operational confirmation represents a critical milestone for future agentic system implementations in space environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Comparative Discussion</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The discrepancy between ground and on-orbit results underscores an important limitation of agentic LLM-based systems in flight conditions: when the environment’s dominant timescale (e.g., orbital sun/eclipse transitions) is comparable to or shorter than the LLM inference latency, semantic supervision can become counterproductive. However, the observed reduction in thermal violations suggests that, even under latency constraints, agentic policies can contribute to improved stability in certain operational metrics. This finding highlights a general principle for the deployment of LLMs in safety-critical space systems: they are unsuitable for direct real-time intervention unless the latency of decision-making is significantly smaller than the dynamics of the controlled process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, these mixed results are highly informative. They provide empirical evidence that complements the successful ground experiments and establish practical design guidelines.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Implications for Future Space Autonomy</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experimentation confirms that the integration of linguistic models for semantic supervision of other processes is feasible in flight-qualified equipment within the transformative New Space era. Agentic systems are thus positioned as a viable and, given their inherent characteristics, transformative approach to integrating control logic into traditional subsystems. The deployment of ASTREA demonstrates how agentic systems can serve as supervisory layers for traditional control architectures. By combining linguistic reasoning with adaptive control, ASTREA exemplifies a scalable approach to onboard autonomy, capable of supporting fault detection, mission planning, and subsystem coordination.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given the analytical capabilities that extend beyond rigid rule-based systems, it is reasonable to anticipate that the integration of agentic systems aboard spacecraft will become the norm rather than the exception in the near future. While these systems may not assume complete mission control, they are well-positioned to assist other subsystems, collaborate with additional LLM agents or other agent types as proposed in this study, or simply analyze and characterize emergency levels from satellite telemetry to provide early warnings to ground control systems. Furthermore, an agentic system could autonomously plan onboard analyses of sensor data using specialized models or corroborate telemetry through alternative methods utilizing other sensors typically present on the satellite, mirroring the approach an engineer would take when confronted with anomalous telemetry readings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This capability assumes even greater significance in remote exploration missions, providing the ability to perform contextual analysis while applying pseudo-reasoning to decision-making processes, thereby conferring a high degree of automation and autonomy to space systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This type of onboard analysis and automation would be impractical to achieve with other technologies. Even implementing a fraction of such analytical capability would require extensive development and validation periods under simulated extreme conditions, inevitably excluding parameters not considered during the design and implementation phases of such software. This limitation eliminates any capacity for improvisation, which is often precisely what is needed in highly dynamic and unpredictable environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">However, incorporating the most powerful linguistic models into flight-qualified hardware remains technically unfeasible at present. This constraint necessitates maintaining short context lengths in prompts and structuring them in such a way that smaller models, compatible with current flight hardware capabilities, can properly contextualize the information received in the prompt and respond appropriately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Another critical consideration in the deployment of such systems is the grounding problem. As Schoepp S. et al. expose, this manifests in the integration of LLM-agents with other systems that expect more structured inputs, such as reinforcement learning agents, requiring the adaptation of natural language generated by the LLM-agent into executable actions for the RL-agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. One approach to address or mitigate these grounding challenges, as implemented in this study’s use case, is to employ the well-established Tool Use design pattern. This approach constrains the model to utilize tools that generate concrete actions, enabling the model itself to perform the necessary adaptation as part of its analytical process for response generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusions and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study addresses the utilization of agentic systems in real flight equipment to provide an advanced layer of pseudo-reasoning at the edge. The research evaluates the utility of small linguistic models, which despite their contextual understanding limitations, remain feasible given current hardware constraints in flight equipment, deployed at the edge for autonomous control applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">ASTREA represents a pioneering implementation of agentic autonomy in flight-qualified hardware. By integrating a quantized LLM with a reinforcement learning agent, ASTREA establishes a framework for semantic supervision and adaptive control in space systems. Its modular design and asynchronous architecture offer a scalable blueprint for future multi-agent deployments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To validate this approach, we experimented with the integration of an LLM-agent with a reinforcement learning agent previously employed in earlier studies for autonomous thermal control of an edge processing node through the management of available processing resources. While the models present inherent limitations, our findings demonstrate that through the application of various optimization techniques, their utilization becomes viable, yielding clear improvements in the thermal control agent’s performance, during the cold start phase, by modifying its behavior based on obtained results. This highlights the potential of linguistic models for long-term context analysis, efficiently complementing the capabilities of reinforcement learning models in decision-making processes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The present study employed a single LLM agent with significant response time limitations. This constraint would become even more critical in multi-agent systems where bottlenecks could easily emerge, making it worthwhile to explore these limitations in future research.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since the flight equipment utilized lacks an accelerator for optimal LLM model execution, deployment occurred on the CPU alongside other concurrent processes. While functionality has been demonstrated, the incorporation of a space-qualified accelerator would fundamentally transform system performance, potentially enabling the use of more complex models or alternative execution designs such as shorter analysis windows and more frequent evaluations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In the analyzed use case, the LLM agent suggests entropy coefficient values for a reinforcement learning model. However, numerous other parameters within such models could also be analyzed by the LLM agent, including the possibility of adjusting the reward function to shape the behavioral objectives of the RL agent, as explored by Navarro T. et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, the capabilities of LLMs are not limited to fine-tuning existing parameters; their potential extends to higher-level control mechanisms. Thanks to their ability to interpret broader contexts, LLM agents could monitor instrument degradation throughout a mission and adapt usage strategies accordingly. They might also detect periodic patterns in environmental conditions based on orbital position and dynamically select among reinforcement learning models with varying characteristics—such as more conservative or more aggressive behaviors. Furthermore, these models could be combined or switched in real time depending on evolving operational priorities. For instance, the system might prioritize energy efficiency over computational performance during specific mission phases, or vice versa, depending on the mission’s current objectives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, this study selected a general-purpose model that may lack sufficient thermal control domain knowledge. Repeating the experiment with a model specialized in thermal control would be highly interesting, as enhanced task-specific knowledge could enable more grounded analysis without requiring extensive prompt engineering to condition its pseudo-reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, ASTREA proved effective in the lab and, in orbit, reduced thermal violations and increased CPU utilization in line with mission goals, although episode durations were shorter. These results confirm the feasibility of LLM‑based supervision in flight and provide concrete design criteria for future spaceborne autonomous systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I am grateful to my colleague Julian Cobos Aparicio for his careful review of the manuscript and for providing valuable editorial suggestions that enhanced its clarity. I also thank my friend and colleague David Alami for introducing me to the field of agentic AI, which inspired the conception of this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
